            <ArticleTitle>Successful treatment of postherpetic neuralgia with oral ketamine.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>240-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To demonstrate the possibilities of the use of oral ketamine in the treatment of postherpetic neuralgia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">A pain clinic in a university hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENT" NlmCategory="METHODS">A patient with postherpetic neuralgia of the ophthalmic nerve.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Subcutaneous and later oral ketamine after classical treatment had failed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A complete recovery was accomplished without any sign of side effects.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Oral ketamine may provide an alternative in the treatment of postherpetic neuralgia. The possible mechanism of action by its N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) blocking properties is discussed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
